re unless pursuing a different shape (e.g., going for Seven Pairs, but I have a meld already, so not Seven Pairs).\n5. **Play T9**: I have a pair of T9 (terminal). Keeping terminal pair is good for potential Pung of Terminals or Outside Hand. Discarding one would break it.\n6. **Play F2**: I have a pair of F2 (Prevailing Wind). This is valuable for points. Discarding one would lose that potential.\n\nReason: Given the need to move toward a winning hand, I should discard tiles that are least useful and keep potential for higher scoring patterns. My hand has:\n- Two pairs of terminals/honors (W9, T9, F2) which could become Pungs for points.\n- One Chow meld in Bamboo.\n- To form additional melds, I need either sequences or sets. The Bamboo suit might be promising because I already have a Chow there and some Bamboo tiles (T2, T4, T9). But T9 is terminal, not easily sequenced. T2 and T4 are not connected well (need T1/T3 or T3/T5). Alternatively, I could aim for a hand with honors and terminals, using Pungs of W9, T9, F2, and maybe another meld. But I lack many tiles for that.\n\nConsidering tile safety: Looking at opponents' discards and melds:\n- Next player discarded T1, T2, W4, etc. Not clear.\n- Player across has melds using T1,T2,T3 and W3,W4,W5, so they might be collecting low tiles in those suits.\n- Previous player discarded T4, B1, etc.\n\nDiscarding T4 might be relatively safe as it has been discarded by previous player (Play T4 in history). Discarding T2 might be riskier as player across used T2 in a Chow (Chi T2). W2 hasn't been discarded much.\n\nBest choice: Discard T4 because it's an isolated tile with limited connectivity (T5 is already used in meld, so T4 can't form a Chow with T5,T6), it's relatively safe based on discard history, and it helps reduce clutter while preserving pairs and potential for Pung of honors/terminals. Keeping F2 pair for Prevailing Wind points is important. Keeping W9 and T9 pairs for terminal Pungs also good. So, discard T4.\n\